The instruction is the Web chat row of the table: "Offer two rooms with the free dates shown". The question is whether "two rooms" is a limit on the act, meaning a count of rooms that the rule constrains.

The table contrasts "one room, not two" on WhatsApp with "two rooms" for Email and Web chat. Here the number says how many rooms to offer, so it is a count of an act. The compliant range is exactly two. The schema has no "eq" comparison, so I use "ge" or "le". The sentence reads as exactly two, and I pick "le" or "ge" accordingly. Offering two is the required amount; the more natural reading is that two is the target, so I use "ge" with 2 as a minimum.

{"limits": [{"limit": 2, "comparison": "ge", "role": "constraint", "unit": "rooms offered"}]}

The instruction gives a default phrase to write when no day has been given. "Within two working days" is quoted text that the agent writes, so it states a commitment to the customer rather than a limit on the agent's own act. The agent is not told to respond within two days; it is told what wording to use.

The number two is part of that wording. It is not a deadline, maximum, minimum or count that constrains the agent's behavior. The instruction's only condition is that the front desk has not given a day, and that condition has no number.

